{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1476508539,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDgyNDc2NjU0NA=="},"added":["severity-normal"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1476537155,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1Mzk4MzQ5OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-253983499"},"message":"FTR: failed to replicate in what I thought could have lead to such behavior (recursive save in newly created datasets):\n\n``` shell\n$\u003e d=/tmp/test; sd=$d/sub1; sd2=$sd/sub2; fd=$sd2/d; f=$fd/file.dat; chmod +w -R $d; rm -rf $d; mkdir -p $fd; touch $f; datalad create --force $d; datalad create --force $sd; datalad create --force $sd2; datalad save -m \"saving\" -a -r -d $d; ls -ld $f\n```","files":null},{"type":2,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1476934294,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50ODMwMDEwOTkw"},"title":"auto-add-changes also adds files.  Origin: create saves into super-dataset before it commits to git/annex of the sub","was":"auto-add-changes also adds files.  Origin: create saves into super-dataset before it commits to git/annex of the sub"},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1476934322,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDgzMDAxMTIzOQ=="},"added":["conference agenda item"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1476934329,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1NTAwMDQ0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-255000444"},"message":"ok -- here is the test which replicates it :-/  and it is related to our aggressive default pre-committing changes (related or not, even if not under git/annex at all) \n\n``` python\n@with_tree({'ds2': {'file1.txt': 'some'}})\ndef test_saving_prior(topdir):\n    # the problem is that we might be saving what is actually needed to be\n    # \"created\"\n\n    # we would like to place this structure into a hierarchy of two datasets\n    # so we create first top one\n    ds1 = create(topdir, force=True)\n    # and everything is ok, stuff is not added BUT ds1 will be considered dirty\n    ok_(ds1.repo.dirty)\n    # And then we would like to initiate a sub1 subdataset\n    ds2 = create('ds2', dataset=ds1, force=True)\n    # But what will happen is file1.txt under ds2 would get committed first into\n    # ds1, and then the whole procedure actually crashes since because ds2/file1.txt\n    # is committed -- ds2 is already known to git and it just pukes with a bit\n    # confusing    'ds2' already exists in the index\n```\n\nso if we are to save changes by default -- shouldn't we just save actual \"changes\" not all the files under that dataset, some of which might not even be in the dataset?  and then make it possibly\n`--auto-add=updates|all` (edit) with  `-a` for making it 'all' and `-u` for `updates` so staying in line with git behavior? @mih @bpoldrack -- what do you think?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1476957929,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1NTA2NDQwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-255064408"},"message":"I think, this would lead to going away from the goal to provide a tool, that makes things easier for non-git people. I'd prefer to one of those:\n1. keep `all`, but allow for exclusions. So `create` can exclude that path when saving before. (But: That means, you actually can not restore the state \"before\" completely, which doesn't seem to be desirable)\n2. consider `create` a command, that doesn't provide a `save_before` functionality, since the thing to save the state in, isn't existing yet. I think this is the easiest solution and can absolutely be justified.\n3. creation of a subdataset needs to take that result of `save` into account and move things. More complicated to achieve but best behaviour, I guess.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1476961055,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1NTA3NDY5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-255074698"},"message":"Let me drop a quick additional argument - do we provide a .gitignore oracle? Or in other words, with all top be default behavior, we quickly guarantee that many files added would be junk, especially in mixed source+ code repositories","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1476961370,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1NTA3NTY2Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-255075667"},"message":"I'd argue, that in such a case you should configure a different default default for `--if-dirty` and/or provide a proper .gitignore respectively.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1476961540,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1NTA3NjE1Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-255076156"},"message":"I increasingly feel, that we are building an API in flavor of special cases and git people, instead of aiming for a clean and simple concept for the people we are supposed to target. Defaults should be best for the simple cases and configurable to cover different setups, I think.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1476979969,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1NTE1MjgwOA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-255152808"},"message":"- but we shouldn't alienate git people of which there is an increasing number! ;)\n- making if-dirty configurable I think is a good idea.  I think this is a level where indeed per-user configuration might make sense.  I would still like people to use 'add' ... need to read that gitless paper. But look at their API (http://gitless.com/) -- it has \"track\" and \"untrack\", which is I guess what \"add\", and somewhat of \"remove\" does -- introduces tracking of specific files.  Adding just everything in a directory would just lead to all kinds of junk getting in imho.\n- unlikely we can provide ultimate _proper_ .gitignore but I think we indeed should provide some with most common cases\n\nHere is e.g. a reasonable starting selection from a pool of repos I have on the drive (debian packaging and whatnot, total of 2789 .gitignore files, some are repetetive, coming from the same projects, could improve analysis based on unique files)\n\n``` shell\n$\u003e locate .gitignore | xargs grep -h . 2\u003e/dev/null| grep -v -e '^ *#' -e '\\.\\(png\\|pdf\\|jpg\\|jpeg\\|tgz\\|.gz\\)$'| sort | uniq -c | sort -n | tail -n 50\n     48 configure\n     48 /dist\n     50 config.status\n     50 .mr.developer.cfg\n     51 .ropeproject\n     52 *.debhelper*\n     52 tmp\n     54 *.pyo\n     56 .pc\n     57 bin\n     57 *.substvars\n     59 .idea\n     59 *.orig\n     60 /Makefile.in\n     61 *.exe\n     61 files\n     65 /*~\n     66 Makefile.in\n     68 nosetests.xml\n     72 .#*\n     74 *.mo\n     75 .o\n     76 .installed.cfg\n     77 /build\n     81 *.egg\n     81 MANIFEST\n     82 .tox\n     83 Makefile\n     87 pip-log.txt\n     88 vmlinux.lds\n     94 .pydevproject\n    100 *.py[cod]\n    108 *.a\n    110 dist/\n    123 *.bak\n    126 *.egg-info\n    127 build/\n    127 !.gitignore\n    133 .project\n    136 *\n    140 dist\n    142 *.log\n    172 *.o\n    199 build\n    213 *.swp\n    215 .coverage\n    217 .DS_Store\n    218 *.so\n    349 *.pyc\n    356 *~\n```\n\ninteresting hit is `*`!  apparently often ppl ignore EVERYTHING unless otherwise explicitly added (with --force probably).  Quite frequently they have \n\n```\n*\n!.gitignore\n```\n\nwhich hints on the fact that some git-savvy ppl might prefer to complete avoid any magical 'add' in favor of explicit statements.  After all, explicit (add) is better that implicit (especially hidden under \"updates\" word)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1478035289,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI1NzcwMTc2Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-257701767"},"message":"I thought we have already dealt with this issue, but apparently we didn't one way (to have auto-add-changes add actual changes not new content, which would make creation of subdatasets within present content possible, see above)  or another...  and I have ran into it again :-/","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1484580668,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI3Mjg5MjQxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-272892417"},"message":"Added the proposed test and a slightly more complete form to #1169 -- no longer an issue.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1484580675,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDkyNDQ4MjYyNQ=="},"added":["fix-implemented"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1484765565,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI3MzU2NTUzNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1016#issuecomment-273565534"},"message":"Fix merged.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1484765565,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50OTI3OTU3NjIz"},"status":2}]}